# Copy to /etc/arrosage/caddy-basic-auth.env on the Raspberry Pi (never commit real values).
#
# systemd EnvironmentFile does not use shell expansion, so a value like $2a$14$... is
# fine here. Do not `source` this file in bash (especially with set -u): $2 would be
# treated as the second positional parameter.
#
# 1. Generate a bcrypt hash (interactive avoids leaving the password in shell history):
#        caddy hash-password
#    Paste the password at the prompt; copy the line starting with $2a$ or $2y$.
#
# 2. On the Pi, create the env file with exactly one assignment (no "export" prefix —
#    systemd EnvironmentFile expects KEY=value only; no quotes needed):
#        ARROSAGE_BASIC_AUTH_HASH=$2a$14$.........................................
#
# 3. Caddy runs as user jnfrm; the file must be readable by that user, e.g.:
#        sudo install -m 0640 -o root -g jnfrm /etc/arrosage/caddy-basic-auth.env
#    or: sudo chown root:jnfrm /etc/arrosage/caddy-basic-auth.env && sudo chmod 640 ...
#
# 4. Run: sudo ./deploy/install-caddy.sh
#
# Docs: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/basicauth
